1
00:00:01,535 --> 00:00:03,505
Hello, this is Café Hikouki.

2
00:00:03,505 --> 00:00:05,133
<i>I'm sorry.</i>

3
00:00:05,133 --> 00:00:08,333
<i>Mitsuru isn't coming back anymore.</i>

4
00:00:10,705 --> 00:00:14,105
Just come back, Mr. Seo.

5
00:00:24,333 --> 00:00:27,965
[Episode 4]

6
00:00:27,965 --> 00:00:31,169
Starting now, all prepared foods
are selling at half price.

7
00:00:31,169 --> 00:00:33,105
Would you like to get anything for dinner?

8
00:00:33,105 --> 00:00:34,833
Go ahead.

9
00:00:35,885 --> 00:00:37,593
- Oh!
- Oh.

10
00:00:41,705 --> 00:00:44,361
They just started selling these at half price
right when I came over here.

11
00:00:45,229 --> 00:00:47,309
This one's very good, I recommend it.

12
00:00:47,309 --> 00:00:49,625
- Ah, chili shrimp?
- Yes.

13
00:00:49,625 --> 00:00:52,517
- All right, maybe I'll get it.
- Sure.

14
00:00:54,705 --> 00:00:59,645
The password for his phone
was the wedding date.

15
00:00:59,645 --> 00:01:03,889
So it was your wedding ceremony date,
not your wedding anniversary?

16
00:01:03,889 --> 00:01:08,861
No, not ours.
It's someone else's wedding day.

17
00:01:09,609 --> 00:01:11,781
Huh?

18
00:01:11,781 --> 00:01:15,669
I met Mitsuru at the wedding
of one of my friends.

19
00:01:15,669 --> 00:01:17,909
Oh, so that was the day you two first met?

20
00:01:17,909 --> 00:01:18,961
Yes.

21
00:01:18,961 --> 00:01:23,117
So it was such a memorable encounter, huh?

22
00:01:23,117 --> 00:01:27,749
- Well, I suppose so... never mind that.
- I suppose.

23
00:01:27,749 --> 00:01:29,865
And then?

24
00:01:29,865 --> 00:01:34,413
There's nothing about Azusa in his phone.

25
00:01:34,413 --> 00:01:36,748
Not even her contact information.

26
00:01:37,733 --> 00:01:40,713
There weren't any messages
on some payment app?

27
00:01:40,713 --> 00:01:43,073
Did you check the email drafts?

28
00:01:43,073 --> 00:01:44,533
I know.

29
00:01:44,533 --> 00:01:47,457
It seems like
a trendy way of cheating these days.

30
00:01:47,457 --> 00:01:50,441
It's a clever plan, isn't it?

31
00:01:50,441 --> 00:01:52,201
So you checked?

32
00:01:52,201 --> 00:01:53,897
I did.

33
00:01:53,897 --> 00:01:56,585
And there wasn't anything.

34
00:01:56,585 --> 00:02:01,153
The only thing there was my sense of guilt.

35
00:02:02,144 --> 00:02:03,877
I'm sure that wasn't easy.

36
00:02:06,217 --> 00:02:07,957
Also...

37
00:02:08,757 --> 00:02:10,497
Huh?

38
00:02:11,337 --> 00:02:13,099
Also,

39
00:02:14,641 --> 00:02:18,513
- he'd spent more on games than I thought.
- Ah, I see.

40
00:02:18,513 --> 00:02:21,641
Yeah, so that was about it.

41
00:02:24,761 --> 00:02:26,533
Oh, well...

42
00:02:26,533 --> 00:02:29,833
On my end, well... there wasn't
anything phone-related,

43
00:02:29,833 --> 00:02:33,237
but there was something else decisive
that came out.

44
00:02:33,237 --> 00:02:35,273
There were tickets.

45
00:02:36,101 --> 00:02:37,245
Tickets?

46
00:02:37,245 --> 00:02:39,189
Tickets for the aquarium.

47
00:02:39,901 --> 00:02:43,321
Hold... hold on just a second.

48
00:02:43,321 --> 00:02:44,861
Huh?

49
00:02:44,861 --> 00:02:48,345
So you still haven't found the manager?

50
00:02:48,345 --> 00:02:52,381
- That's right.
- He's still so young... what a shame.

51
00:02:53,065 --> 00:02:55,901
Thank you very much. Please come again.

52
00:02:56,517 --> 00:03:00,173
[Thank you as always]

53
00:03:00,173 --> 00:03:03,369
The one who's truly pitiful is Sakiko.

54
00:03:03,369 --> 00:03:06,797
I found these in Mitsuru's shirt.

55
00:03:11,373 --> 00:03:12,725
[48th Musashino Summer Fireworks Festival]
The aquarium...

56
00:03:12,725 --> 00:03:15,217
Maybe she was planning to go with you.

57
00:03:15,217 --> 00:03:18,349
Oh, no, I don't go to aquariums.

58
00:03:18,349 --> 00:03:22,753
I don't like aquariums.
And Azusa knows that very well.

59
00:03:23,373 --> 00:03:26,641
- It's the same for me.
- Oh? So you don't like fireworks...

60
00:03:26,641 --> 00:03:30,177
It's not fireworks exactly.
I don't like fireworks festivals.

61
00:03:30,177 --> 00:03:32,578
And Mitsuru knows that very well.

62
00:03:33,305 --> 00:03:36,985
So... they were probably planning
to go with someone who wasn't us.

63
00:03:39,365 --> 00:03:40,985
[48th Musashino Summer Fireworks Festival]
This feels way more real

64
00:03:40,985 --> 00:03:43,365
[Shinagawa Aquarium]
than a LINE conversation, doesn't it?

65
00:03:43,365 --> 00:03:45,133
It's like a physical evidence.

66
00:03:47,109 --> 00:03:48,841
I'm sorry.

67
00:03:51,381 --> 00:03:55,589
It's coming up soon,
so feel free to go with Sho if you like.

68
00:03:55,589 --> 00:03:58,438
These are tickets for those nice,
extra-charge couple's seats.

69
00:03:58,438 --> 00:04:00,540
Couple's seat?

70
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:04,177
No, no, that's just what they're called.

71
00:04:04,825 --> 00:04:06,713
Then, take these too if you'd like.

72
00:04:06,713 --> 00:04:09,121
It seems like it's valid
until the end of this month.

73
00:04:09,121 --> 00:04:10,689
Maybe you can go with a friend.

74
00:04:10,689 --> 00:04:14,965
All right, I'll do that. Thank you very much.

75
00:04:14,965 --> 00:04:18,057
[Shinagawa Aquarium]

76
00:04:18,057 --> 00:04:19,973
If you've finished, you can go ahead
and have your lunch break.

77
00:04:19,973 --> 00:04:21,781
- Okay.
- Chizuru.

78
00:04:21,781 --> 00:04:24,413
Do you have any plans for this Saturday?
Sunday would be fine too.

79
00:04:24,413 --> 00:04:25,801
- This weekend?
- Yes.

80
00:04:25,801 --> 00:04:27,057
Will you listen to this?

81
00:04:27,057 --> 00:04:28,702
Oh, it's fine if you already have plans.

82
00:04:28,702 --> 00:04:32,469
I get to spend a whole weekend with him
for the first time.

83
00:04:32,469 --> 00:04:33,865
His wife is really very tolerant.

84
00:04:33,865 --> 00:04:36,573
I see, that's great.

85
00:04:36,573 --> 00:04:38,353
I hope you have a great time.

86
00:04:39,409 --> 00:04:42,141
What do you usually do on your days off?

87
00:04:42,141 --> 00:04:44,333
Well, recently...

88
00:04:45,865 --> 00:04:47,988
I'm mostly spending time with Mr. Sonoda.

89
00:04:47,988 --> 00:04:52,285
- Oh really? That's great, isn't it?
- No, it's not like that.

90
00:04:52,285 --> 00:04:55,553
There's always the option
of leaving Mitsuru, you know.

91
00:04:57,465 --> 00:05:01,085
But I just thought you should know
that's an option too.

92
00:05:01,085 --> 00:05:02,909
Just take it easy and wait for now.

93
00:05:18,513 --> 00:05:22,425
I'm happy to help,
so if there's anything, just let me know.

94
00:05:23,285 --> 00:05:26,449
You're really pushing yourself too hard.

95
00:05:26,449 --> 00:05:30,721
We're all very worried about you,
and we feel bad for you.

96
00:05:30,721 --> 00:05:31,870
It'd actually be strange

97
00:05:31,870 --> 00:05:34,517
if you could work at a time like this
without making mistakes.

98
00:05:34,517 --> 00:05:38,237
Everyone understands,
so please feel free to rely on us more.

99
00:05:38,237 --> 00:05:39,797
Please do that.

100
00:05:39,797 --> 00:05:42,249
All right. Thank you.

101
00:06:03,433 --> 00:06:08,565
[3-4-4 Minamisensu, Minami-Shinshu,
Nagano Prefecture]

102
00:06:20,849 --> 00:06:24,177
[Shinagawa Aquarium]

103
00:06:38,881 --> 00:06:40,673
Look, a dolphin!

104
00:06:42,725 --> 00:06:45,417
Wow, so cute!

105
00:07:07,537 --> 00:07:09,373
Sorry, sorry, sorry.

106
00:07:15,853 --> 00:07:19,273
We've come here together before.

107
00:07:19,273 --> 00:07:20,845
That's right.

108
00:07:21,449 --> 00:07:23,517
It was right after we got married.

109
00:07:24,441 --> 00:07:26,929
We went to see the dolphins, didn't we?

110
00:07:26,929 --> 00:07:29,473
We both got soaking wet.

111
00:07:29,473 --> 00:07:31,573
We sure did.

112
00:07:32,373 --> 00:07:37,001
It was this hot time of year,
and the place was packed with families.

113
00:07:37,001 --> 00:07:38,717
Yes.

114
00:07:39,965 --> 00:07:42,801
We were saying things like,
"Next time let's come as a family of three."

115
00:07:47,745 --> 00:07:51,293
I wonder if a 5-year-old boy
would like something like this?

116
00:07:53,253 --> 00:07:55,421
I wonder.

117
00:07:56,385 --> 00:07:58,621
Or maybe he'd prefer this?

118
00:07:58,621 --> 00:08:00,369
Hmm...

119
00:08:05,461 --> 00:08:09,449
I didn't get a chance to ask him
why he doesn't like aquariums.

120
00:08:09,449 --> 00:08:12,905
He didn't ask you why
you don't like fireworks festivals either.

121
00:08:15,893 --> 00:08:17,617
He's very nice.

122
00:08:29,261 --> 00:08:31,301
Where did you call from?

123
00:08:36,105 --> 00:08:38,393
Are you staying at that address?

124
00:08:39,717 --> 00:08:40,857
Oh, sorry!

125
00:08:40,857 --> 00:08:42,889
- Are you all right?
- I'm fine.

126
00:09:05,977 --> 00:09:08,781
It's to see Sho... it's to see Sho...

127
00:09:08,781 --> 00:09:12,749
I can put up with others...
I can put up with others...

128
00:09:13,697 --> 00:09:15,425
Hello.

129
00:09:16,137 --> 00:09:18,557
Welcome back.

130
00:09:18,557 --> 00:09:20,637
Would you like Napolitan spaghetti for lunch?

131
00:09:20,637 --> 00:09:23,849
Ever since you were little,
for lunch on Saturdays,

132
00:09:23,849 --> 00:09:27,945
- you'd always sing, "Napolitan".
- That was Miki who said that.

133
00:09:27,945 --> 00:09:31,153
- Though I do like it too.
- She was just saying that imitating you.

134
00:09:31,153 --> 00:09:34,333
- Miki prefers udon noodles.
- Oh, I see.

135
00:09:35,537 --> 00:09:39,679
- Daddy! Grrr!
- Fist bump!

136
00:09:39,679 --> 00:09:42,049
What's this? Did you get someone
to buy something for you again?

137
00:09:42,049 --> 00:09:44,206
I went to the aquarium.

138
00:09:44,206 --> 00:09:45,242
Huh?

139
00:09:45,242 --> 00:09:47,725
We went on a weekday
because it's crowded on weekends.

140
00:09:47,725 --> 00:09:50,221
I figured you were busy with work,
so I didn't ask you to join us.

141
00:09:50,221 --> 00:09:51,957
Here, carry this for me.

142
00:09:52,673 --> 00:09:54,425
Here you go.

143
00:09:57,793 --> 00:10:00,166
- Mom.
- Yes?

144
00:10:00,166 --> 00:10:03,129
You really know everything
that Miki and I like, don't you?

145
00:10:03,129 --> 00:10:05,021
Well, of course. I'm your mom.

146
00:10:05,021 --> 00:10:07,712
But you don't seem to know
what we don't like, do you?

147
00:10:07,712 --> 00:10:09,569
Thanks for the food.

148
00:10:16,353 --> 00:10:18,481
- Hello?
- <i>Hi, it's me. Sorry to bother you.</i>

149
00:10:18,481 --> 00:10:21,289
<i>I called because I wanted an answer
right away. I'm sorry.</i>

150
00:10:21,289 --> 00:10:22,769
What's the matter?

151
00:10:22,769 --> 00:10:25,529
Well, I'm here at the aquarium,

152
00:10:25,529 --> 00:10:28,729
and I want to get something for Sho,

153
00:10:28,729 --> 00:10:30,588
but is it okay to get something for him

154
00:10:30,588 --> 00:10:33,317
that might remind you
of a place you don't like?

155
00:10:33,317 --> 00:10:36,249
<i>But originally, Sho... well, for example...</i>

156
00:10:36,249 --> 00:10:38,539
<i>It makes more sense for him to come here
with someone like your younger sister,</i>

157
00:10:38,539 --> 00:10:41,061
<i>but instead,
I ended up swapping tickets with you...</i>

158
00:10:41,061 --> 00:10:43,089
So I feel like I should give him
a gift to apologize.

159
00:10:43,089 --> 00:10:44,581
To begin with,

160
00:10:44,581 --> 00:10:46,781
I know your family
has its own way of raising children,

161
00:10:46,781 --> 00:10:48,189
but I got a bit carried away the other day

162
00:10:48,189 --> 00:10:49,474
and I gave you things like that curry bread.

163
00:10:49,474 --> 00:10:51,821
But since then, I've really reflected...

164
00:10:51,821 --> 00:10:54,013
Thank you for your thoughtfulness.

165
00:10:54,013 --> 00:10:55,053
<i>No, no, it's nothing.</i>

166
00:10:55,053 --> 00:10:58,601
- Well... then sharks...
- Sharks?

167
00:10:58,601 --> 00:11:01,461
There are all kinds of sharks here!

168
00:11:01,461 --> 00:11:05,433
<i>Please get something
that isn't a shark plush toy.</i>

169
00:11:05,433 --> 00:11:07,517
Something that isn't?

170
00:11:07,517 --> 00:11:08,749
I see.

171
00:11:08,749 --> 00:11:11,569
I suppose even round ones like this
are still sharks after all,

172
00:11:11,569 --> 00:11:13,437
<i>and quite scary.</i>

173
00:11:13,437 --> 00:11:16,417
<i>Thank goodness! I was just about to buy it.</i>

174
00:11:16,417 --> 00:11:17,873
Okay, thank you very much.

175
00:11:17,873 --> 00:11:21,329
Oh, and what about sweets?
There are all kinds of them here.

176
00:11:21,329 --> 00:11:22,565
<i>I'll leave that up to you.</i>

177
00:11:22,565 --> 00:11:25,084
Please get anything that you like.

178
00:11:27,086 --> 00:11:30,501
Let's eat it together
while we wait for the laundry.

179
00:11:31,749 --> 00:11:32,818
All right.

180
00:11:34,853 --> 00:11:38,157
Oh, I'll handle this one.

181
00:11:38,157 --> 00:11:39,913
Look, a shark.

182
00:11:41,697 --> 00:11:44,370
Looks like you had a good time.

183
00:11:44,370 --> 00:11:48,613
I watched the dolphin show
and the sea lion show twice each.

184
00:11:48,613 --> 00:11:51,685
And I also watched the penguins walk around.

185
00:11:51,685 --> 00:11:54,493
That's great.

186
00:11:54,493 --> 00:11:58,429
- Yes. Thank you for the tickets.
- No, no, thank you for the gifts.

187
00:11:59,405 --> 00:12:04,149
Places that have those kinds of events
really are very bright and lively, right?

188
00:12:04,149 --> 00:12:07,025
- Yes.
- And if it's a place like that,

189
00:12:08,801 --> 00:12:11,108
there are those places in an aquarium

190
00:12:11,108 --> 00:12:13,573
that really feel like an aquarium, right?

191
00:12:13,573 --> 00:12:16,365
Like where you just
watch fish swimming around.

192
00:12:16,365 --> 00:12:18,933
- A huge tank that takes up an entire wall?
- Yes, exactly.

193
00:12:18,933 --> 00:12:23,121
- Those kinds of places are dimly lit.
- Yes, that's true.

194
00:12:23,121 --> 00:12:25,136
When I was about Sho's age,

195
00:12:25,136 --> 00:12:28,157
I got lost when I went to the aquarium
with my family.

196
00:12:28,157 --> 00:12:32,593
Oh, I see! Getting lost in a dimly lit place
like that would be quite scary.

197
00:12:32,593 --> 00:12:34,965
Well, but actually the scariest part

198
00:12:34,965 --> 00:12:38,688
was when my mom found me.

199
00:12:38,688 --> 00:12:43,196
She found me huddled up
in a corner, sobbing quietly,

200
00:12:43,196 --> 00:12:45,333
and she scoffed at me as she said,

201
00:12:45,333 --> 00:12:47,585
"You were the one who said
you wanted to come here, right?

202
00:12:49,037 --> 00:12:51,393
You wanted to come here so badly,
so why the tears?

203
00:12:51,393 --> 00:12:53,665
Then maybe we shouldn't have come
in the first place."

204
00:12:53,665 --> 00:12:57,473
I was endlessly lectured like that,
and in a way that I wasn't expecting.

205
00:12:57,473 --> 00:13:03,349
As I walked along that dimly lit passage,
I really felt like it was all my fault.

206
00:13:05,257 --> 00:13:08,117
I wonder why it isn't possible for
someone else to take a parent's place.

207
00:13:09,641 --> 00:13:12,693
It would be nice if it was like with tickets,

208
00:13:13,453 --> 00:13:16,228
where we could just trade them away
if they were something we didn't like.

209
00:13:16,933 --> 00:13:21,165
Oh? So do you feel the same way
about your family?

210
00:13:21,165 --> 00:13:22,881
Yes.

211
00:13:22,881 --> 00:13:26,305
That's how I was able to hit it off
with Mitsuru and become close to him.

212
00:13:27,117 --> 00:13:29,897
I suppose we're getting carried away
talking about people we don't get along with.

213
00:13:31,173 --> 00:13:34,317
What about Azusa?
Was there anything she didn't like?

214
00:13:34,317 --> 00:13:35,521
Financier.

215
00:13:35,521 --> 00:13:39,453
- Oh? You mean sweets?
- No, she liked sweets and baked goods,

216
00:13:39,453 --> 00:13:41,405
but it seems she really
didn't like Financier.

217
00:13:41,405 --> 00:13:44,957
Really? I can't really tell the difference
between those and that other one.

218
00:13:44,957 --> 00:13:46,272
- You mean madeleines?
- Yes, madeleines.

219
00:13:46,272 --> 00:13:48,273
I know, right? I can't tell either.

220
00:13:48,273 --> 00:13:50,609
But anyway, she seemed to like madeleines.

221
00:13:50,609 --> 00:13:55,177
So you understood that about her, right?
That she only disliked financiers.

222
00:13:55,177 --> 00:13:57,889
Yes. What about Mitsuru?

223
00:13:57,889 --> 00:13:59,913
- Pumpkin.
- Oh, right.

224
00:14:00,745 --> 00:14:02,253
What about you?

225
00:14:02,253 --> 00:14:03,989
Me? Cucumbers.

226
00:14:03,989 --> 00:14:06,177
It's basically just water, isn't it?

227
00:14:06,177 --> 00:14:07,488
Hey, that's not very nice.

228
00:14:07,488 --> 00:14:09,641
You shouldn't laugh at people
for things they don't like.

229
00:14:09,641 --> 00:14:11,465
- What don't you like?
- Tomatoes.

230
00:14:11,465 --> 00:14:14,137
- Isn't that just water too?
- No, it's not!

231
00:14:14,137 --> 00:14:15,722
What about ketchup?

232
00:14:15,722 --> 00:14:18,517
I'm fine as long as it isn't raw tomatoes.

233
00:14:18,517 --> 00:14:20,385
I'm actually quite fond of
Napolitan spaghetti.

234
00:14:20,385 --> 00:14:22,633
Oh, that's good.

235
00:14:22,633 --> 00:14:24,221
What do you mean?

236
00:14:24,221 --> 00:14:25,981
Oh, nothing.

237
00:14:28,693 --> 00:14:32,401
So do you think you'll be able to
go to the fireworks festival with Sho?

238
00:14:32,401 --> 00:14:34,861
Yes. And listen to this.

239
00:14:34,861 --> 00:14:38,921
Starting next week,
I'll be able to bring Sho back here.

240
00:14:38,921 --> 00:14:40,768
Really? That's great!

241
00:14:40,768 --> 00:14:42,633
Yes, finally.

242
00:14:42,633 --> 00:14:44,884
I made it through the most grueling thing
in the world,

243
00:14:44,884 --> 00:14:47,717
a conversation with my mother.

244
00:14:47,717 --> 00:14:50,297
- Well done.
- Thank you.

245
00:14:50,297 --> 00:14:52,529
- That's really great.
- Hello.

246
00:14:52,529 --> 00:14:54,529
- Hello.
- Hello.

247
00:14:55,325 --> 00:14:57,581
Oh, it's someone else.

248
00:14:58,673 --> 00:15:00,416
There's a man and woman
I sometimes see here together,

249
00:15:00,416 --> 00:15:02,468
but I haven't seen them recently.

250
00:15:04,153 --> 00:15:05,881
Huh?

251
00:15:05,881 --> 00:15:08,889
The woman was always the one
who came up to talk to me,

252
00:15:08,889 --> 00:15:11,249
and it left an impression on me.

253
00:15:11,249 --> 00:15:12,629
Are they a couple?

254
00:15:12,629 --> 00:15:16,549
Well... I'm not sure, but I don't think so.

255
00:15:17,378 --> 00:15:21,225
- I suppose they didn't seem that close...
- No, they did seem close.

256
00:15:21,225 --> 00:15:23,169
They seemed like family.

257
00:15:26,161 --> 00:15:28,305
Maybe they moved away.

258
00:15:30,149 --> 00:15:32,201
Yes, maybe.

259
00:15:41,653 --> 00:15:43,785
Married couples are family too.

260
00:15:45,521 --> 00:15:46,624
Yes.

261
00:15:52,777 --> 00:15:54,929
Sorry.

262
00:15:54,929 --> 00:15:58,725
- I haven't done this in a while.
- No, it's fine. This is a big help.

263
00:16:00,445 --> 00:16:05,297
Naoto, can you make that by yourself
without checking with Mitsuru?

264
00:16:05,297 --> 00:16:07,985
Yes, I can. And I have the recipe too.

265
00:16:07,985 --> 00:16:12,089
Mitsuru took care of everything with
the wholesalers by himself, didn't he?

266
00:16:12,089 --> 00:16:14,407
Wouldn't that be tough?

267
00:16:14,407 --> 00:16:16,433
Well, just in case something were to happen,

268
00:16:16,433 --> 00:16:17,592
he left a notebook on how to do those,

269
00:16:17,592 --> 00:16:20,133
one that's easy to read and understand.

270
00:16:21,297 --> 00:16:23,173
In case something were to happen?

271
00:16:24,129 --> 00:16:28,353
- So he said that, and left that notebook?
- He meant if he ever wasn't feeling well.

272
00:16:32,121 --> 00:16:33,174
Excuse me.

273
00:16:40,037 --> 00:16:42,813
Hello, this is Café Hikouki.

274
00:16:44,089 --> 00:16:48,925
Yes, that's right,
we're still closed on Fridays.

275
00:16:48,925 --> 00:16:50,961
Yes. Please do come again.

276
00:17:00,081 --> 00:17:01,781
Hey.

277
00:17:01,781 --> 00:17:06,773
Is there anyone you know
in Minamisensu, in Nagano?

278
00:17:07,673 --> 00:17:09,721
Nagano?

279
00:17:09,721 --> 00:17:11,721
No, I don't think so.

280
00:17:15,025 --> 00:17:17,473
- Sakiko.
- Yes?

281
00:17:17,473 --> 00:17:19,621
Are you eating properly?

282
00:17:21,169 --> 00:17:22,901
Why do you ask?

283
00:17:27,197 --> 00:17:31,429
Why are you so concerned about me, Naoto?

284
00:17:32,509 --> 00:17:35,487
Because Mr. Seo is concerned about you.

285
00:17:39,113 --> 00:17:41,805
When Mr. Seo comes back,

286
00:17:41,805 --> 00:17:46,645
I think he will be worried
if you aren't your usual energetic self.

287
00:17:51,957 --> 00:17:54,973
I'm getting plenty to eat.

288
00:17:56,433 --> 00:17:59,585
And also, the position of this graph
might be reversed.

289
00:17:59,585 --> 00:18:00,846
Ah, I'm sorry.

290
00:18:00,846 --> 00:18:02,413
- Okay, do you think you can fix it?
- Understood.

291
00:18:02,413 --> 00:18:04,605
- Then please do that as soon as possible.
- Thank you.

292
00:18:08,717 --> 00:18:10,625
What... What is it?

293
00:18:10,625 --> 00:18:15,573
No, it's just that I'm amazed
how you're able to work so normally.

294
00:18:15,573 --> 00:18:18,037
Oh? Did you think
I wasn't very good at my job?

295
00:18:18,037 --> 00:18:22,369
No, no. It's just that
my pet hamster died last year.

296
00:18:22,369 --> 00:18:24,838
And ever since then, I've been feeling kind
of lightheaded,

297
00:18:24,838 --> 00:18:26,881
and I keep making mistakes.

298
00:18:26,881 --> 00:18:30,153
It took me about six months
to feeling like myself again.

299
00:18:30,849 --> 00:18:32,969
I see.

300
00:18:32,969 --> 00:18:34,565
That must have been tough.

301
00:18:34,565 --> 00:18:36,024
It's still tough,

302
00:18:36,024 --> 00:18:38,841
but I've shifted my mindset
and I'm getting on with work again.

303
00:18:38,841 --> 00:18:43,173
- Thank you for pointing out my mistake.
- Yes, please go ahead.

304
00:18:47,225 --> 00:18:51,681
- What is it?
- She bought a new hamster right away.

305
00:18:51,681 --> 00:18:54,585
And it's funny, she even gave it
the same name. Hamurin.

306
00:18:54,585 --> 00:18:56,369
So, it's a replacement?

307
00:18:57,621 --> 00:19:01,409
You know, you look so totally unfazed
that I actually worry about you.

308
00:19:01,409 --> 00:19:03,569
And everyone was kind of
tiptoeing around it, I guess.

309
00:19:05,413 --> 00:19:07,609
You should use your paid leave
and go somewhere with your son.

310
00:19:07,609 --> 00:19:09,089
I've suggested you many places, haven't I?

311
00:19:09,089 --> 00:19:11,984
Oh, but tomorrow night,
I'll be going to a fireworks festival.

312
00:19:17,393 --> 00:19:21,677
[Mom: I'm on a trip with Sho]

313
00:19:26,257 --> 00:19:29,568
- All right, I'll leave it to you.
- Sure.

314
00:19:38,125 --> 00:19:40,117
<i>Sorry for the short notice,</i>

315
00:19:40,117 --> 00:19:44,441
<i>is there anyone else you might want to
give the fireworks festival tickets to?</i>

316
00:19:47,665 --> 00:19:52,229
<i>I just found out that my parents
made Sho go on a trip with them.</i>

317
00:19:53,101 --> 00:19:56,261
<i>I think this might be
my mom's way of being spiteful.</i>

318
00:19:59,065 --> 00:20:03,245
[I see. Well, you don't have to
force yourself to go...]

319
00:20:03,245 --> 00:20:05,221
<i>I'm free,</i>

320
00:20:05,221 --> 00:20:09,113
<i>but I don't have anyone to go with me,
so I was wondering what I should do.</i>

321
00:20:29,133 --> 00:20:33,825
[Would it be all right if we go together?]

322
00:20:38,437 --> 00:20:41,465
[Sure]

323
00:21:20,905 --> 00:21:24,613
Mrs. Seo? Mrs. Seo?

324
00:21:26,573 --> 00:21:28,277
What is it? Are you all right?

325
00:21:30,573 --> 00:21:33,369
- I'm sorry.
- No, no, it's okay. Can you stand?

326
00:21:35,901 --> 00:21:38,733
- I'm fine.
- Oh, just take your time... okay.

327
00:21:46,541 --> 00:21:49,401
- Do you feel a bit calmer now?
- Yes.

328
00:21:49,401 --> 00:21:51,541
Did you get overheated or something?

329
00:21:51,541 --> 00:21:54,617
No, I'm fine.
I just feel sick from the crowd.

330
00:21:54,617 --> 00:21:56,965
Oh, I see.
There certainly are a lot of people here.

331
00:21:58,449 --> 00:22:00,553
Wait, so you didn't actually want to come,
did you?

332
00:22:00,553 --> 00:22:04,092
No, no, I've wanted to come for years.

333
00:22:04,092 --> 00:22:07,005
Even though I was scared, I wanted to come.

334
00:22:07,005 --> 00:22:08,573
And this is a good opportunity.

335
00:22:08,573 --> 00:22:11,957
You showed up at just the right time,
and that really helped.

336
00:22:11,957 --> 00:22:13,745
No, no, it was nothing.

337
00:22:16,209 --> 00:22:18,397
I'm okay now. Let's go.

338
00:22:21,661 --> 00:22:23,629
No, let's not go.

339
00:22:32,181 --> 00:22:35,865
I think we should be able to see
the fireworks from over there.

340
00:22:35,865 --> 00:22:39,789
- Although it is a bit of a distance. Sorry.
- No, no, it's fine.

341
00:22:39,789 --> 00:22:41,805
Thank you.

342
00:22:44,261 --> 00:22:46,309
But why?

343
00:22:46,309 --> 00:22:48,681
[East 3rd Parking Garage]
Well, I did a bit of research,

344
00:22:50,289 --> 00:22:54,097
and I thought that maybe
you don't like big crowds.

345
00:22:54,933 --> 00:22:57,609
Sorry, I know this isn't the nicest location.

346
00:22:58,881 --> 00:23:01,129
- Thank you.
- It's fine.

347
00:23:02,197 --> 00:23:04,782
Well, I did kind of want to see

348
00:23:04,782 --> 00:23:07,365
what the view would be like
from those couple's seats.

349
00:23:08,497 --> 00:23:09,889
Too bad.

350
00:23:09,889 --> 00:23:12,857
Okay, let me take them back from you.

351
00:23:12,857 --> 00:23:14,641
Sorry.

352
00:23:14,641 --> 00:23:16,728
Thank you.

353
00:23:20,349 --> 00:23:22,493
If it's okay with you,
we could have a drink...

354
00:23:22,493 --> 00:23:25,689
Oh, sorry...
that wasn't very thoughtful of me.

355
00:23:25,689 --> 00:23:29,193
Do you drink alcohol?
I wasn't sure if you're someone who drinks,

356
00:23:29,193 --> 00:23:31,545
so I brought along all kinds of...

357
00:23:31,545 --> 00:23:33,641
I see. Then what about you?

358
00:23:34,825 --> 00:23:37,977
I used to drink every day.

359
00:23:37,977 --> 00:23:39,965
Oh, I see.

360
00:23:39,965 --> 00:23:42,297
Right? It's the same with me.

361
00:23:43,417 --> 00:23:46,285
- Since the accident?
- Right. I haven't drunk alcohol since then.

362
00:23:46,285 --> 00:23:49,857
I guess I just feel a bit of resistance

363
00:23:51,009 --> 00:23:54,113
toward indulgences or doing things
just for enjoyment.

364
00:23:54,757 --> 00:23:56,549
I get it.

365
00:23:57,349 --> 00:23:59,289
But what about smoking?

366
00:23:59,949 --> 00:24:02,361
I suppose that is an indulgence, isn't it?

367
00:24:04,141 --> 00:24:06,421
- All right, shall we have our drinks now?
- Sure.

368
00:24:06,421 --> 00:24:09,357
- Are you okay with beer?
- Sure, thank you.

369
00:24:11,750 --> 00:24:14,285
- What is it?
- Just a gesture that Mitsuru used to make,

370
00:24:14,285 --> 00:24:15,360
one that I liked.

371
00:24:15,360 --> 00:24:16,957
Why are you bringing that up all of a sudden?

372
00:24:16,957 --> 00:24:20,309
He would open a can like this
with just one hand, with a "pssh" sound.

373
00:24:20,309 --> 00:24:21,856
Hey, even I can do something like that.

374
00:24:21,856 --> 00:24:24,885
No, the way he did it without even looking
was so cool.

375
00:24:24,885 --> 00:24:26,861
You really like him, don't you?

376
00:24:28,717 --> 00:24:30,449
But,

377
00:24:31,161 --> 00:24:34,149
I think I've mostly cleared away
my filter now.

378
00:24:34,969 --> 00:24:38,037
I'm seeing so many aspects of Mitsuru
that I didn't know about before.

379
00:24:38,037 --> 00:24:39,921
Are you talking about the "loved one filter"?

380
00:24:41,185 --> 00:24:43,062
That was kind of scary,

381
00:24:43,062 --> 00:24:45,589
the way you just suddenly
changed completely that time.

382
00:24:45,589 --> 00:24:48,441
The way you said that to me,
"You should clean it."

383
00:24:48,441 --> 00:24:51,529
No, no...
you were just being way too innocent.

384
00:24:51,529 --> 00:24:53,965
- And I felt sorry for you.
- Hey, don't be mean!

385
00:24:55,109 --> 00:24:57,937
- Anyway, whatever. Let's drink.
- Sure.

386
00:24:59,333 --> 00:25:01,805
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

387
00:25:07,617 --> 00:25:09,769
- That's good!
- Ahhh.

388
00:25:10,749 --> 00:25:13,599
It feels so luxurious,
drinking outside like this.

389
00:25:13,599 --> 00:25:15,181
It sure is nice, isn't it?

390
00:25:15,181 --> 00:25:16,917
Yeah.

391
00:25:22,893 --> 00:25:24,965
That's not good, right?

392
00:25:26,029 --> 00:25:28,809
People like us
shouldn't be doing things like this...

393
00:25:30,793 --> 00:25:32,933
Do you mean coming to a fireworks festival

394
00:25:33,785 --> 00:25:36,249
and drinking and laughing like this?

395
00:25:37,629 --> 00:25:39,353
Yes.

396
00:25:41,257 --> 00:25:43,321
What does it mean to be in mourning?

397
00:25:44,569 --> 00:25:47,741
Does it mean crying all the time?

398
00:25:48,761 --> 00:25:51,333
Does working flawlessly

399
00:25:51,333 --> 00:25:54,241
mean I'm someone
who's unfazed if their wife passed away?

400
00:25:56,717 --> 00:25:58,753
Sorry.

401
00:25:58,753 --> 00:26:01,557
It's just that at work...

402
00:26:06,329 --> 00:26:09,313
In my third year after joining my company,

403
00:26:10,621 --> 00:26:17,241
my boss, who was in his 50s at the time,
lost his wife to an illness.

404
00:26:17,241 --> 00:26:21,765
He was feeling pretty down for quite a while,
but then, after about six months,

405
00:26:21,765 --> 00:26:25,569
he suddenly became all bright and cheerful.

406
00:26:25,569 --> 00:26:30,797
I thought that maybe he'd picked up
a hobby or something, but...

407
00:26:34,737 --> 00:26:37,517
Had he found someone new?

408
00:26:37,517 --> 00:26:38,814
Wow, amazing.

409
00:26:41,277 --> 00:26:43,013
Yes, that's right.

410
00:26:43,745 --> 00:26:46,234
It seemed that he'd found someone new.

411
00:26:46,234 --> 00:26:50,241
I was so glad that he looks so happy.

412
00:26:50,241 --> 00:26:51,945
But my colleagues,

413
00:26:52,965 --> 00:26:55,769
there were a lot of people
who were disillusioned.

414
00:26:55,769 --> 00:26:58,749
It was like, "Oh, so that's how things were
between him and his wife."

415
00:27:02,141 --> 00:27:04,173
I wonder what that was all about.

416
00:27:05,893 --> 00:27:07,906
Did they think a person who faced tragedy

417
00:27:09,110 --> 00:27:13,133
should stay unhappy forever?

418
00:27:13,985 --> 00:27:18,777
Love was the reason why he felt better.

419
00:27:18,777 --> 00:27:21,425
There are many who are harsh about that
when it comes to others.

420
00:27:26,369 --> 00:27:28,325
So, I...

421
00:27:31,757 --> 00:27:33,601
No, we...

422
00:27:35,553 --> 00:27:40,433
How long do we have to be pitiful like this
until it's okay to be happy?

423
00:27:45,105 --> 00:27:46,853
Well...

424
00:27:50,537 --> 00:27:53,077
Wow!

425
00:27:53,077 --> 00:27:56,365
It's been a long time since I last saw
a perfectly round fireworks like that!

426
00:27:56,365 --> 00:28:01,557
Wow, amazing! It sure is beautiful, isn't it?

427
00:28:01,557 --> 00:28:03,541
Oh, sorry. What is it?

428
00:28:03,541 --> 00:28:05,801
No, it's nothing. Let's enjoy the fireworks.

429
00:28:07,105 --> 00:28:08,945
- Whoa!
- Wow, did you see that one?

430
00:28:08,945 --> 00:28:14,165
- Yes, I did. I'm right here in front.
- Wow, amazing!

431
00:28:16,445 --> 00:28:19,561
<i>The fireworks I hadn't seen in so long
were just too beautiful.</i>

432
00:28:20,557 --> 00:28:23,901
<i>And the person to whom I'd want to show
something this beautiful would be</i>

433
00:28:24,717 --> 00:28:27,793
<i>someone very important to me, I thought.</i>

434
00:28:29,425 --> 00:28:31,565
<i>And because I thought that,</i>

435
00:28:33,273 --> 00:28:35,609
<i>I was glad to have someone next to me.</i>

436
00:28:37,897 --> 00:28:40,409
<i>It's good to have someone
who's in an equally tough situation</i>

437
00:28:42,253 --> 00:28:44,417
<i>to be here together with me.</i>

438
00:28:49,321 --> 00:28:51,392
When I was in high school,

439
00:28:51,392 --> 00:28:54,201
I went to a fireworks festival
with my boyfriend.

440
00:28:55,029 --> 00:28:57,469
I wanted him to see me wearing a cute yukata,

441
00:28:57,469 --> 00:28:59,181
so I invited him.

442
00:28:59,181 --> 00:29:02,605
It's okay, you don't have to force yourself
to talk about it if you don't want to.

443
00:29:03,569 --> 00:29:07,021
On the way to the venue,
it was incredibly crowded,

444
00:29:07,021 --> 00:29:08,789
and how should I say this?

445
00:29:09,953 --> 00:29:13,717
It wasn't just crowded.
It also felt like something was off.

446
00:29:14,753 --> 00:29:18,385
To put it simply, someone groped me.

447
00:29:18,385 --> 00:29:22,269
Though I'd rather not just brush it off
with a single remark.

448
00:29:24,749 --> 00:29:27,361
It was scary, and I couldn't talk about it.

449
00:29:27,361 --> 00:29:29,621
And my boyfriend hadn't realized anything.

450
00:29:29,621 --> 00:29:32,008
I finally managed to find some open space,

451
00:29:32,008 --> 00:29:34,241
but whoever did that to me had disappeared.

452
00:29:35,069 --> 00:29:37,577
I was on the verge of tears
when I told him what had happened,

453
00:29:39,133 --> 00:29:40,933
and he was very angry.

454
00:29:40,933 --> 00:29:42,848
He said, "Why didn't you tell me right away?"

455
00:29:42,848 --> 00:29:43,877
I see.

456
00:29:44,989 --> 00:29:48,921
Well, my boyfriend at the time
was just a 17-year-old kid.

457
00:29:49,813 --> 00:29:52,238
So anyway, I was crying,

458
00:29:52,238 --> 00:29:54,993
and I left by myself
before the fireworks had even started.

459
00:29:56,293 --> 00:29:59,345
On the way back it was even more crowded,

460
00:29:59,345 --> 00:30:02,193
and I was so scared I couldn't even move.

461
00:30:03,685 --> 00:30:06,657
Then a policeman rescued me
and called my parents.

462
00:30:07,797 --> 00:30:09,597
It was a total disaster.

463
00:30:11,885 --> 00:30:13,821
That must have been really scary.

464
00:30:17,105 --> 00:30:20,057
Right? He just had to say,
"That must have been really scary".

465
00:30:21,993 --> 00:30:26,301
Yes. That's all I wanted him to say.

466
00:30:27,593 --> 00:30:31,117
My mom was excessively protective of me,

467
00:30:31,117 --> 00:30:34,725
and she assumed
that he'd forced me to go out with him,

468
00:30:34,725 --> 00:30:37,665
- and showed up unannounced at his house...
- Whoa.

469
00:30:37,665 --> 00:30:41,021
That really was the worst.

470
00:30:42,361 --> 00:30:45,933
And then, before getting married,

471
00:30:45,933 --> 00:30:47,424
I kept it a secret

472
00:30:47,424 --> 00:30:50,229
and went to a fireworks festival
with Mitsuru.

473
00:30:50,229 --> 00:30:53,569
But I really couldn't handle the crowds,

474
00:30:53,569 --> 00:30:56,785
and when I told him the story of
what had happened to me before,

475
00:30:57,845 --> 00:31:00,865
he said to me,
"That must have been really scary."

476
00:31:00,865 --> 00:31:03,109
- What is it?
- Oh, nothing.

477
00:31:03,109 --> 00:31:07,629
We agreed to be more open
about what we don't handle well,

478
00:31:07,629 --> 00:31:09,577
and then we got married.

479
00:31:10,493 --> 00:31:12,937
Wow, that's pretty incredible.

480
00:31:12,937 --> 00:31:14,725
- What?
- Oh, no, it's just...

481
00:31:15,713 --> 00:31:21,221
You have a knack for turning every story
into how much you love your husband.

482
00:31:24,221 --> 00:31:26,277
Thank you very much.

483
00:31:27,113 --> 00:31:29,813
Sorry if I caused any trouble.

484
00:31:29,813 --> 00:31:32,269
No, no. I also...

485
00:31:33,661 --> 00:31:35,441
had a good time.

486
00:31:40,273 --> 00:31:43,409
All right then, good night.

487
00:31:43,409 --> 00:31:45,121
Good night.

488
00:31:57,421 --> 00:32:00,149
[To Sakiko Seo]

489
00:32:08,029 --> 00:32:11,761
[Minamisensu]

490
00:32:16,168 --> 00:32:19,645
- I'm home.
- Welcome back.

491
00:32:19,645 --> 00:32:21,397
Welcome back for real.

492
00:32:22,085 --> 00:32:25,161
There really is no place like home.

493
00:32:26,377 --> 00:32:28,337
You sound like your mom.

494
00:32:29,217 --> 00:32:31,701
- Make sure to wash your hands and gargle.
- Okay.

495
00:32:43,161 --> 00:32:45,865
It's been a while
since the three of us were all here together.

496
00:32:45,865 --> 00:32:48,189
It's because you said that
you were feeling lonely.

497
00:32:50,117 --> 00:32:54,121
I explained to Sho what happened to you.

498
00:32:55,153 --> 00:32:57,257
I wonder if he understood.

499
00:32:58,529 --> 00:33:00,681
- Did he understand?
- Yes.

500
00:33:01,737 --> 00:33:03,473
He cried.

501
00:33:05,545 --> 00:33:08,329
I think he basically understood.

502
00:33:09,613 --> 00:33:12,037
And today, he's full of energy again,
just like that.

503
00:33:13,077 --> 00:33:16,073
Did you tell him
that I've turned into a star or something?

504
00:33:16,073 --> 00:33:17,801
No.

505
00:33:18,429 --> 00:33:21,841
I said you're dead,
and he won't be able to see you again.

506
00:33:22,641 --> 00:33:24,429
That's good, then.

507
00:33:25,541 --> 00:33:28,201
It's better not to try to gloss things over.

508
00:33:28,201 --> 00:33:29,953
Right.

509
00:33:30,737 --> 00:33:33,865
- What about you?
- Yes?

510
00:33:33,865 --> 00:33:35,633
Do you understand?

511
00:33:37,165 --> 00:33:41,413
I'm dead,
and you won't be able to see me again.

512
00:33:55,413 --> 00:33:58,221
- Did you buy bread for breakfast?
- Yes, I did.

513
00:33:58,221 --> 00:34:00,833
I got six of the round ones.

514
00:34:00,833 --> 00:34:03,485
I want to eat breakfast bread at night.

515
00:34:03,485 --> 00:34:06,521
No, you have to eat breakfast bread
in the morning.

516
00:34:07,457 --> 00:34:09,641
- Oh, look.
- Sakiko!

517
00:34:09,641 --> 00:34:13,241
- Hello.
- Oh hi, Sho.

518
00:34:13,241 --> 00:34:15,845
- Oh, starting today?
- Yes.

519
00:34:16,901 --> 00:34:18,673
Come with us.

520
00:34:19,661 --> 00:34:22,877
No, she's not coming with us.
Mrs. Seo is going out now.

521
00:34:22,877 --> 00:34:25,585
- Come with us.
- She isn't coming.

522
00:34:27,005 --> 00:34:30,097
Hey, you're putting Mrs. Seo in a bind here.
Come on, let go.

523
00:34:30,893 --> 00:34:33,029
They say my mom died.

524
00:34:34,297 --> 00:34:36,981
We're missing one person, so come with us.

525
00:34:41,305 --> 00:34:43,009
Let's go home.

526
00:34:46,873 --> 00:34:48,621
Do you love your mom?

527
00:34:49,437 --> 00:34:52,309
I see. That's great.

528
00:34:52,309 --> 00:34:54,993
- And I love my dad too.
- Wow, that's the best.

529
00:34:54,993 --> 00:34:56,709
I'm jealous.

530
00:34:58,425 --> 00:35:05,245
I couldn't possibly replace your mom,
not even as a substitute.

531
00:35:06,185 --> 00:35:10,242
But if you ever want to play with me,

532
00:35:10,242 --> 00:35:13,677
or have a meal together,
then feel free to let me know.

533
00:35:13,677 --> 00:35:15,609
- And I'll be right with you.
- Okay.

534
00:35:18,977 --> 00:35:20,381
Sho.

535
00:35:20,381 --> 00:35:23,297
I want to have a meal together.

536
00:35:27,337 --> 00:35:29,877
I just made a promise.

537
00:35:29,877 --> 00:35:31,680
Would it be all right
if I make something for us to eat together?

538
00:35:31,680 --> 00:35:33,505
Of course.

539
00:35:33,505 --> 00:35:35,385
- What would you like to eat tonight?
- Omurice!

540
00:35:35,385 --> 00:35:38,369
Oh, good. I can make that.

541
00:35:38,369 --> 00:35:40,065
I have some side dishes too.
I'll go get them.

542
00:35:40,065 --> 00:35:43,025
- Oh, sorry for the trouble. Thank you.
- No problem at all.

543
00:36:09,517 --> 00:36:11,765
Thank you very much.
I can take care of the rest.

544
00:36:11,765 --> 00:36:13,473
Ah, okay.

545
00:36:19,677 --> 00:36:21,421
Sorry.

546
00:36:24,709 --> 00:36:26,925
Do you still want to hit him?

547
00:36:26,925 --> 00:36:29,181
Oh, you mean Mitsuru?

548
00:36:30,237 --> 00:36:32,881
Do you still have that feeling
of wanting to hit him?

549
00:36:32,881 --> 00:36:34,769
Right now, not really.

550
00:36:34,769 --> 00:36:37,677
That thing with the aquarium
kind of took a toll on me.

551
00:36:37,677 --> 00:36:39,989
I'm certain Azusa was planning
to go with someone who wasn't me.

552
00:36:39,989 --> 00:36:42,429
Oh, it's kind of the same thing
with the fireworks festival, right?

553
00:36:42,429 --> 00:36:43,909
Yes.

554
00:36:43,909 --> 00:36:46,981
Is it like a feeling of defeat?

555
00:36:46,981 --> 00:36:50,765
- Yes, a feeling of total defeat.
- Right, there is that feeling.

556
00:36:50,765 --> 00:36:52,489
Yes.

557
00:36:54,385 --> 00:36:59,265
So, I guess
you're done looking for Mitsuru, then?

558
00:36:59,265 --> 00:37:01,705
Well, I don't think
I'll be taking the initiative.

559
00:37:04,333 --> 00:37:06,077
I see.

560
00:37:06,077 --> 00:37:07,792
What is it?

561
00:37:12,301 --> 00:37:15,169
- There was a phone call from Mitsuru.
- What?

562
00:37:15,169 --> 00:37:18,129
But he wasn't calling me,
he was calling the café.

563
00:37:18,789 --> 00:37:21,553
It seems that he's in touch with Naoto.

564
00:37:22,469 --> 00:37:25,821
I happened to answer the phone
when he called.

565
00:37:26,645 --> 00:37:29,609
I thought about how to press him for answers,

566
00:37:29,609 --> 00:37:31,745
but then I was just like,
"Oh well, never mind."

567
00:37:31,745 --> 00:37:33,817
What? No, no,
you should definitely try to press him.

568
00:37:33,817 --> 00:37:35,921
I'm sure there's something that he knows.

569
00:37:37,081 --> 00:37:39,937
Yesterday, this thing...

570
00:37:43,837 --> 00:37:45,829
I got this in the mail.

571
00:37:46,553 --> 00:37:50,905
The sender's name isn't written,
but this is Mitsuru's handwriting.

572
00:37:53,689 --> 00:37:57,209
Minamisensu... where's that?

573
00:37:57,209 --> 00:37:59,269
Nagano.

574
00:37:59,269 --> 00:38:01,457
Oh, so he is still in that area.

575
00:38:01,457 --> 00:38:04,597
The postmark alone doesn't tell us very much.

576
00:38:04,597 --> 00:38:06,817
Well, take a look at what's inside.

577
00:38:06,817 --> 00:38:08,557
All right, then.

578
00:38:11,423 --> 00:38:15,185
[If the dryer isn't working well,
clean the filter. Here]

579
00:38:15,185 --> 00:38:16,929
Huh?

580
00:38:18,817 --> 00:38:20,885
I was like, "Aren't there
way more important things to write about?"

581
00:38:20,885 --> 00:38:24,633
[If the dryer isn't working well,
clean the filter. Here]

582
00:38:27,737 --> 00:38:30,765
I think the reason he's in touch with Naoto

583
00:38:30,765 --> 00:38:34,473
might be that he wants to know how I'm doing.

584
00:38:35,949 --> 00:38:41,886
And by sending me this letter,
he's also telling me that he's doing well,

585
00:38:43,289 --> 00:38:45,769
and that he won't be coming back anymore.

586
00:38:48,769 --> 00:38:53,631
He's telling me
to handle the laundry all by myself.

587
00:38:57,825 --> 00:39:00,897
His drawing is so bad,
it's almost infuriating, isn't it?

588
00:39:09,025 --> 00:39:11,341
It really is infuriating.

589
00:39:13,673 --> 00:39:18,457
Well... if that's what you think,

590
00:39:18,457 --> 00:39:20,601
then I suppose it's true.

591
00:39:22,173 --> 00:39:27,317
We always ended up spending time together,
even on our off days.

592
00:39:29,565 --> 00:39:32,237
It was fun, kind of like playing detective.

593
00:39:33,277 --> 00:39:35,493
Well, maybe "fun" isn't quite the right word.

594
00:39:37,417 --> 00:39:40,145
Being with someone who's
in a similar situation to mine,

595
00:39:40,997 --> 00:39:45,261
I suppose it's made me
feel a bit more at ease.

596
00:39:47,065 --> 00:39:50,885
I know it's only been a very short time,

597
00:39:51,557 --> 00:39:53,353
but thank you very much.

598
00:39:53,353 --> 00:39:57,381
Huh? That sounds like
a final farewell or something.

599
00:39:58,597 --> 00:40:00,333
I see.

600
00:40:02,385 --> 00:40:06,953
Well, I would be very happy if we could
continue our relationship as neighbors.

601
00:40:06,953 --> 00:40:09,245
Me too.

602
00:40:09,245 --> 00:40:11,075
Thank you for having me.

603
00:40:15,985 --> 00:40:18,629
- Then...
- Yes?

604
00:40:19,809 --> 00:40:22,013
Maybe now,

605
00:40:22,013 --> 00:40:24,393
we can put an end to our sadness,

606
00:40:25,029 --> 00:40:27,141
and be happy.

607
00:40:35,085 --> 00:40:36,809
Maybe. I wonder.

608
00:40:40,865 --> 00:40:42,593
All right, see you.

609
00:40:46,173 --> 00:40:47,911
Good night.

610
00:40:57,481 --> 00:41:04,473
How long do we have to be pitiful like this
until it's okay to be happy?

611
00:41:05,233 --> 00:41:07,009
Well...

612
00:41:10,385 --> 00:41:12,768
For pitiful people like us,
wouldn't it be better like this now?

613
00:41:12,768 --> 00:41:15,929
It's been a long time since I last saw
a perfectly round fireworks like that!

614
00:41:15,929 --> 00:41:19,137
Wow, amazing!

615
00:41:33,357 --> 00:41:35,085
I'm sorry, Azusa.

616
00:41:52,045 --> 00:41:54,349
[3-4-4 Minamisensu, Minami-Shinshu,
Nagano Prefecture]

617
00:41:59,505 --> 00:42:01,981
[Nagano Prefecture]

618
00:42:06,997 --> 00:42:09,993
[3-4-4 Minamisensu, Minami-Shinshu,
Nagano Prefecture]

619
00:42:54,613 --> 00:42:57,769
[3-4-4 Minamisensu, Minami-Shinshu,
Nagano Prefecture]

620
00:43:46,289 --> 00:43:49,421
Hello, my name is Sakiko Seo.

621
00:43:49,421 --> 00:43:51,853
Is Mitsuru at home?
